\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Across the visualizations, 
\f0\b hitting efficiency was strongly associated with Duke\'92s success
\f1\b0 . In sets Duke won, the team hit 
\f0\b .329
\f1\b0  compared with 
\f0\b .184
\f1\b0  in sets it lost, while also recording more kills and fewer attack errors. Opponents showed the reverse pattern, hitting much more efficiently in Duke losses.\
The 
\f0\b site comparison
\f1\b0  showed that location alone did not explain performance. Duke\'92s hitting efficiency and win percentage varied across home, away, and neutral matches, suggesting that other factors beyond venue were more important in determining outcomes.\
The hitter-specific analysis revealed important differences between players. 
\f0\b Zo\'eb Gillen-Malveaux and Avery Hamlin showed the largest increases in hitting efficiency in Duke wins compared with losses
\f1\b0 , suggesting their offensive performance was particularly associated with team success. In contrast, 
\f0\b Breonna Goss remained highly efficient regardless of match outcome
\f1\b0 .\
Finally, the volume-versus-efficiency scatter plot showed a distinction between 
\f0\b high-volume attackers and highly efficient attackers
\f1\b0 . Nataly Moravec and Ashby Zubchevich handled the largest attacking workloads, while Goss and Gillen-Malveaux produced higher hitting percentages on fewer attempts. Overall, Duke\'92s offense appears to depend on both 
\f0\b high-volume production and efficient complementary attackers
\f1\b0 , rather than a single player alone.\
